<section style="padding:3rem 0;border-top:1px solid var(--color-border)">
<div style="max-width:900px;margin:0 auto;padding:0 1.5rem">
<h2 style="font-size:clamp(1.5rem,3vw,2rem);font-weight:600;color:var(--color-text);margin-bottom:1.5rem">
Why Work with a Bangladesh Clothing Manufacturer?
</h2>
<p style="color:var(--color-text-muted);line-height:1.8;margin-bottom:1.5rem">
Denim jacket production rewards a manufacturer that treats washing as a core
discipline, not an outsourced afterthought. Bangladesh's denim cluster around
Dhaka and Chittagong has grown up around exactly that: in-house washing plants
running stonewash, enzyme wash, and garment dye alongside the cut-and-sew lines,
so a jacket's finish is controlled by the same factory that built it.
</p>
<p style="color:var(--color-text-muted);line-height:1.8;margin-bottom:1.5rem">
That washing capability matters more for jackets than for basics — a denim
jacket's value is mostly in its finish and hardware, not just the fabric. SDF
Clothing runs a 300-piece-per-style minimum, holds GOTS and OEKO-TEX certification
for the fabric itself, and sources buttons, rivets, and zip pulls locally rather
than importing hardware on a separate lead time.
</p>
<p style="color:var(--color-text-muted);line-height:1.8;margin-bottom:1.5rem">
Denim-specific expertise is concentrated in Bangladesh in a way that's harder to
find at small MOQs elsewhere: indigo rope-dyeing, selvedge weaving, and multi-stage
wash development (sandblasting alternatives, laser whiskering, tinting) are
established processes here, not experimental ones. A jacket typically takes
35 to 50 days from approved sample to shipment, factoring in wash trials.
</p>
<p style="color:var(--color-text-muted);line-height:1.8;margin-bottom:1.5rem">
On landed cost, US brands sourcing denim jackets from Bangladesh sidestep the
Section 301 tariffs applied to Chinese-origin goods, while EU buyers get GSP
duty-free treatment on qualifying denim categories. For a garment where hardware,
